llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
121; needs 103, 111 and 113): the single-token matrix-vector products that
read the same input run as one launch.
gfx803 only: the grouped kernel uses the launch shape of 103's GCN4 table;
other GPUs keep one launch per product.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches"), and a small kernel adds
its own ramp-up and drain.

Every layer of Qwen3.6 35B-A3B starts with several products of the same
normalised hidden state. A Gated DeltaNet layer runs qkv (8192 rows), z
(4096), alpha (32, with 113's ssm_dt add) and beta (32); an attention layer
runs q (8192), v (512) and k (512). With 111 they share one q8_1 copy of
the input, but each is still a launch of its own, and the graph places them
far apart (in a DeltaNet layer alpha comes 23 nodes after qkv and z after
the GATED_DELTA_NET). The 32-row products are almost all fixed cost: 16
one-wave blocks that take 5-7 us, plus the dispatch. That is 110 launches
per token (30 x 3 + 10 x 2) that carry no work of their own.

All of these weights are Q4_K or Q6_K in this model, and on gfx803 patch
103 runs both with one warp over two rows per block, so one grid can hold
all of a layer's products with the work per row unchanged.

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in
system RAM with -ncmoe 99, tg128, 5 repetitions, page cache dropped before
each run, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1, three alternating
rounds): 24.64 / 24.58 / 24.64 -> 24.86 / 25.12 / 25.31 tok/s (+2.0%).
Every decode token runs 40 groups, 110 launches fewer. llama-perplexity
with -ub 1 (every token through the decode path) gives the same per-chunk
values as the build without this patch, greedy 128-token output is
byte-identical, and the same holds with 118-121 applied as well.

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_group_shared_input_products, called from graph_optimize before
the allocator sees the graph, moves each later single-token product of the
same src1 to right after the first one (up to four products, within 96
nodes), together with the RESHAPE and ADD of 113's chain where a product
has one. A product reads only its weights and src1, so it can run earlier
unless a node in between writes into src1 or produces one of its
operands; the scan stops at a node that writes into src1. Products that
the MUL_MAT -> ADD bias fusion or the GLU gate/up fusion take stay where
they are, and nothing moves when GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_OPT=1 interleaves q, k
and v over streams. The allocator then places the moved outputs by the new
order.

ggml_cuda_try_fuse_mmvq_group then finds the adjacent group in the
evaluation loop and launches mul_mat_vec_q_group: blocks are numbered over
the concatenated row ranges, and each block takes its weight pointer,
type, row stride, output and optional bias from its segment. A member is a
one-token MUL_MAT of a contiguous Q4_K or Q6_K weight that ggml_cuda_mul_mat
would send to mul_mat_vec_q, on a device with the GCN4 parameter table; the
outputs may not overlap each other, src1 or a bias, and anything else runs
as before. The q8_1 copy of src1 is made or reused exactly as
ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q does (111's kept input, which after 116's MoE tail
saves the group's quantize as well). GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_FUSION=1 turns this
off with the other fusions.

Numerics are unchanged: each block runs the body of
mul_mat_vec_q<type, 1, ...> at the shape 103 selects for these types (one
warp, two rows per block, no small-K variant): the same walk over the
quant blocks per lane, the same vec_dot, the same warp reduction, and for
113's chain the bias added once after the reduction, as the fused kernel
adds it. On gfx803 the group kernel's floating-point instructions are
those of the separate Q4_K and Q6_K kernels (v_mac and v_mad, no v_fma)
plus the two bias adds, and it uses 31 VGPRs against 36-40.

